(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-4-renal-physiology-2-quiz"]={"title":"Renal II: Tubular Transport & Regulation — Quiz (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Where does reabsorption of filtrate first occur as it moves through the nephron?","choices":["The glomerular capsule region","Distal convoluted tubule","Thin descending limb","Proximal convoluted tubule"],"answer":3,"exp":"The proximal convoluted tubule is where filtrate first flows after the glomerular capsule and where reabsorption first occurs, moving material across the apical membrane back into blood. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What determines transport across the collecting duct, according to this lecture's nephron segment overview?","choices":["It transports only solutes and never permits any water transport","Water can be transported across only in the presence of certain hormones","Transport there is determined solely by cortical osmolality","It is always freely permeable to water, hormone-independent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Water can be transported across the collecting duct only in the presence of certain hormones (i.e., it is hormone-dependent, primarily via ADH). — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What determines transport in the distal convoluted tubule, per this lecture's nephron segment overview?","choices":["The medulla's osmolality","Blood pressure alone","Hormone presence exclusively, with no osmotic contribution","The osmolality of the cortex"],"answer":3,"exp":"The osmolality of the cortex determines transport in the distal convoluted tubule. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Per the summary table of nephron segments, which segment shows high water and high NaCl reabsorption with the key role of 'bulk reabsorption'?","choices":["Thin descending limb segment","Early distal convoluted tubule","Thick ascending limb segment","Proximal convoluted tubule (PCT)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The PCT shows high water and high NaCl reabsorption, with 'bulk reabsorption' as its key physiologic role. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which nephron segment is described as functionally similar to the thick ascending limb, impermeable to water, and the 'diluting' segment?","choices":["Collecting duct","Late distal tubule","Early distal tubule","Proximal straight tubule"],"answer":2,"exp":"The early distal tubule functions like the thick ascending limb in some ways, is not permeable to water (making it the 'diluting' segment), and actively reabsorbs Na+, Cl-, K+, and Mg++. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Approximately what percentage of the filtered NaCl load is normally reabsorbed in the early distal tubule?","choices":["~5%","~90%","~65%","~25%"],"answer":0,"exp":"About 5% of the filtered NaCl load is normally reabsorbed in the early distal tubule. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"The juxtaglomerular apparatus is composed of which three structures?","choices":["Afferent arteriole, efferent arteriole, and Bowman's capsule structures","Macula densa, granular (JG) cells, and extraglomerular mesangial cells","Proximal tubule cells, loop of Henle cells, and collecting duct cells","Principal cells, intercalated cells, and glomerular podocytes"],"answer":1,"exp":"The juxtaglomerular apparatus is composed of the macula densa, granular (JG) cells, and extraglomerular mesangial cells. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Where is the juxtaglomerular apparatus located relative to the nephron?","choices":["At the glomerular capsule exclusively, near Bowman's space","At the very start of the proximal convoluted tubule segment","At the very tip of the loop of Henle deep within the medulla","At the end of the thick ascending limb / beginning of the distal convoluted tubule"],"answer":3,"exp":"The JGA is located at the end of the thick ascending limb and the beginning of the distal convoluted tubule. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Granular (JG) cells of the JGA are best described as:","choices":["Specialized neurons belonging to the enteric nervous system","Modified smooth muscle cells that sense perfusion pressure and release renin","Modified epithelial cells that primarily secrete protective mucus","Podocyte-derived cells specialized to filter plasma proteins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Granular (JG) cells are modified smooth muscle cells that release renin, and (as part of the broader JGA response) contribute to sensing perfusion. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"In tubuloglomerular feedback, what happens to renin release when NaCl delivery to the macula densa decreases?","choices":["Renin release is unaffected","Renin is converted directly to angiotensin II without release","Renin release decreases","Renin release increases"],"answer":3,"exp":"Decreased NaCl delivery to the macula densa increases renin release; conversely, increased NaCl delivery suppresses renin. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which of the following increases aldosterone secretion?","choices":["A decrease in plasma K+","Atrial natriuretic factor (ANF)","Angiotensin II","Increased Na+ concentration (osmolality)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Angiotensin II, along with increased K+ levels and ACTH, increases aldosterone secretion. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Aldosterone increases H+ secretion in which cell type of the collecting tubule/duct?","choices":["Principal cells","Renal podocyte cells","Intercalated cells","Mesangial cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Aldosterone increases H+ secretion specifically in intercalated cells, while increasing Na+ reabsorption and K+ secretion in principal cells. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Angiotensin II's role in urine formation includes constricting efferent arterioles and increasing filtration fraction. What does this ultimately cause?","choices":["Complete and total cessation of glomerular filtration","Na+ and water loss, with waste product accumulation","Na+ and H2O retention, maintaining excretion of waste products","Only water retention, with no effect on sodium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Angiotensin II's actions (efferent constriction, decreased peritubular capillary hydrostatic pressure, increased peritubular colloid osmotic pressure) cause Na+ and H2O retention while still maintaining excretion of waste products. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"ANP (atrial natriuretic peptide) is released from the atria in response to:","choices":["Decreased plasma sodium","Decreased blood pressure only, independent of volume","Decreased blood volume","Increased blood volume"],"answer":3,"exp":"ANP is released from the atria when blood volume increases. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"ANP dilates the afferent arteriole and constricts the efferent arteriole. What is the net effect on GFR?","choices":["GFR decreases","GFR is unaffected","GFR becomes unmeasurable","GFR increases"],"answer":3,"exp":"By dilating the afferent arteriole and constricting the efferent arteriole, ANP increases glomerular hydrostatic pressure and increases GFR. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"ADH is secreted by which structure, and what is its primary site of action?","choices":["Secreted by the anterior pituitary gland; acts on the proximal tubule","Secreted by the posterior pituitary; acts on the late distal tubule and collecting duct","Secreted by the adrenal cortex; acts on the thick ascending loop of Henle","Secreted by the kidney tissue itself; acts directly on the glomerulus"],"answer":1,"exp":"ADH is secreted by the posterior pituitary and increases water permeability and reabsorption at the late distal tubule and collecting duct. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Using the lecture's shorthand summary, which hormone 'adds sodium'?","choices":["ANP hormone","ADH hormone","Aldosterone","PTH hormone"],"answer":2,"exp":"In the lecture's mnemonic summary, aldosterone 'adds sodium' (drives Na+ reabsorption). — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Reduced perfusion pressure that triggers RAAS activation is detected by baroreceptors located in the:","choices":["Vasa recta","Efferent arteriole","Afferent arteriole","Distal convoluted tubule epithelium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Reduced perfusion pressure is detected by baroreceptors in the afferent arteriole, one of the three triggers for renin release alongside reduced Na+ delivery and sympathetic stimulation. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Angiotensinogen, the precursor in the RAAS cascade, is produced by which organ?","choices":["Lung","Liver","Kidney","Adrenal cortex"],"answer":1,"exp":"Angiotensinogen is produced in the liver. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"In the RAAS sequence, what converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II?","choices":["Aldosterone (adrenal hormone)","Renin (proteolytic enzyme)","ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme)","ADH (antidiuretic hormone)"],"answer":2,"exp":"ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) converts angiotensin I into angiotensin II. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Full RAAS activation results in vasoconstriction of the efferent arterioles, Na+ reabsorption, increased ADH, and:","choices":["Suppression of the thirst center permanently","Increased thirst","No change in thirst sensation","Decreased thirst"],"answer":1,"exp":"RAAS activation results in increased thirst, alongside efferent arteriole vasoconstriction, Na+ (and water) reabsorption, and increased ADH. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"In the countercurrent multiplier's 'Single Effect' step, what happens in the thin/thick ascending limb?","choices":["Water is passively reabsorbed into the surrounding interstitium","Urea is actively secreted into the tubular lumen at this site","Sodium is actively transported into the interstitium, concentrating it","The segment suddenly becomes highly permeable to water molecules"],"answer":2,"exp":"The 'Single Effect' is active transport of sodium out of the ascending limb into the interstitium, concentrating the interstitial fluid. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"In the 'Fluid Displacement' step of the countercurrent multiplier, water moves out of the thin descending limb because of:","choices":["The active secretion of potassium directly into the descending limb lumen","The higher sodium concentration in the interstitium created by the ascending limb","Active transport of water directly by the descending limb epithelium itself","Aquaporin-2 channel insertion triggered by circulating ADH hormone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Water is drawn out of the water-permeable thin descending limb by the higher sodium concentration in the interstitium (created by the ascending limb's active sodium transport). — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"As tubular fluid travels down the descending limb and then up the ascending limb, its osmolality:","choices":["Only changes in the ascending limb, never the descending limb","Becomes less concentrated going down, then more concentrated going up","Becomes more concentrated going down, then less concentrated going up","Stays constant throughout"],"answer":2,"exp":"Fluid becomes more concentrated as it travels down the descending limb (iso-osmotic going in, increasingly concentrated) and less concentrated (hypo-osmotic) as it travels up the ascending limb. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"The vasa recta, as a countercurrent exchanger, functions to:","choices":["Create the corticomedullary gradient from scratch, independent of the loop","Maintain the corticomedullary gradient while supplying blood to the medulla","Actively transport sodium ions directly out of the tubular fluid","Serve as the primary anatomical site of ADH hormone secretion"],"answer":1,"exp":"The vasa recta maintains (preserves) the corticomedullary gradient established by the loop of Henle, while still supplying blood to the medulla. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Per the lecture's osmolality table, tubular fluid osmolality at the loop tip is approximately:","choices":["~3000 mOsm/kg","~100 mOsm/kg","~300 mOsm/kg","~1200 mOsm/kg"],"answer":3,"exp":"At the loop tip (the deepest point of the loop of Henle), tubular fluid osmolality reaches approximately 1200 mOsm/kg. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which equation correctly relates excretion to the basic mechanisms of urine formation?","choices":["Excretion = Reabsorption − Filtration + Secretion","Excretion = Filtration − Reabsorption + Secretion","Excretion = Secretion − Filtration − Reabsorption","Excretion = Filtration + Reabsorption − Secretion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Excretion = Filtration − Reabsorption + Secretion — what's filtered, minus what's reabsorbed back into blood, plus what's actively secreted into the tubule. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Reabsorption of water, glucose, amino acids, Na+, Cl-, and HCO3- occurs throughout the renal tubules but especially in which segment?","choices":["Collecting duct exclusively","Loop of Henle exclusively","Proximal convoluted tubule (PCT)","Distal convoluted tubule exclusively"],"answer":2,"exp":"Reabsorption of these substances occurs throughout the tubules but especially in the proximal convoluted tubule. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which transporters are found in principal cells of the late distal tubule and collecting duct?","choices":["Carbonic anhydrase and NH3 transporter only","ENaC, ROMK, Na+/K+ ATPase","Aquaporin-1 and Na+/glucose cotransporter only","H+ ATPase, H+/K+ ATPase, Cl-/HCO3- exchanger"],"answer":1,"exp":"Principal cells contain ENaC, ROMK, and Na+/K+ ATPase, supporting their function of Na+ reabsorption, ADH-dependent H2O reabsorption, and K+ secretion. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Type A intercalated cells are responsible for which acid-base function?","choices":["H+ secretion and HCO3- reabsorption","HCO3- secretion alone, with no H+ secretion","ADH-dependent water reabsorption","Na+ reabsorption exclusively"],"answer":0,"exp":"Type A intercalated cells secrete H+ and reabsorb HCO3-, generating new bicarbonate in the process. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 56"}]};
